{
 "id": "2026-zhao-peony",
 "name": "Peony Gardens and Stress Recovery (360° scenes)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the 24 panoramic photographs from Figure 2 of Zhao, Ma, Zhang, Hu & Zhang (2026), Frontiers in Psychology 17: 1935645 (CC BY 4.0), six scenes each for a mountainous, terraced and flatland peony garden and a windowless indoor office (control). After a stress task, each participant is randomly assigned to one setting and views its six 360° scenes for one minute each in random order (6 minutes, as in the paper; drag to look around), then completes the Perceived Restorativeness Scale on the paper's −2 to +2 scale. Stress is rated 0–10 at baseline, after the task and after viewing. The paper found all three peony gardens more restorative than the office, with the mountainous garden highest. Differences from the original study: (1) online with flat 360° images instead of a VR headset, and no heart-rate or EEG recording; (2) the 6-minute Trier Social Stress Test is replaced by its 3-minute serial-subtraction part (count back from 1022 in steps of 13, restart on error); (3) the licensed STAI-S and POMS are replaced by a single 0–10 stress rating; (4) the paper's 23-item PRS is not printed, so the 16-item English wording from Frontiers in Public Health 10:801453 (Table 5) is used; (5) the photographs are small crops of the published figure (561×280 px).",
